/* Generated by tools/build_content.py - do not edit by hand */
PNT.registerModule({"id": "m09", "number": 9, "title": "PNT Verification, Validation and Assurance", "subtitle": "Turning PNT requirements and resilience claims into objective evidence", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Distinguish verification, validation and assurance, and explain how they support different engineering decisions.", "Use Inspection, Analysis, Demonstration and Test (IADT) to select proportionate verification methods.", "Trace mission needs and PNT requirements to verification activities, acceptance criteria and retained evidence.", "Define suitable reference truth for position, velocity and timing measurements.", "Plan nominal, boundary, degraded, denial, deception, failover, holdover and recovery evaluation scenarios.", "Define measurable observables including error, uncertainty, availability, integrity state, detection time, failover time and recovery time.", "Explain why static and dynamic evaluation provide complementary evidence for resilient PNT.", "Use the PNT dependency chain to define verification scope and identify where evidence is required for external services, interfaces and shared dependencies.", "Assess coverage across threats, environments, configurations, sources, interfaces and operational modes.", "Recognise the importance of reproducibility, calibration, configuration control, logging and provenance.", "Build a simple PNT assurance argument linking claims to evidence, assumptions and residual risk.", "Challenge supplier test reports and resilience claims using evidence-led engineering questions."], "lessons": [{"id": "from-engineering-claims-to-evidence", "title": "From Engineering Claims to Evidence", "html": "<p>Module 8 translated mission need into measurable PNT requirements and an architecture. Module 9 asks the next question: what evidence would convince a technically informed reviewer that the implemented system actually meets those requirements in its intended environment, including when PNT is degraded, denied or misleading?</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The assurance question</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>What evidence demonstrates that the required PNT capability is correct, trustworthy and sufficiently resilient for the stated use case, threat conditions and operating environment?</p></div></aside>\n<p>A successful nominal demonstration is not enough. Assurance must consider the requirement, the verification method, the reference truth, the test conditions, the observables, the acceptance criteria, the configuration tested, the limitations of the evidence and the operational context in which the claim is made.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Distinguish verification, validation and assurance, and explain how they support different engineering decisions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use Inspection, Analysis, Demonstration and Test (IADT) to select proportionate verification methods.</p></li>\n<li><p>Trace mission needs and PNT requirements to verification activities, acceptance criteria and retained evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p>Define suitable reference truth for position, velocity and timing measurements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Plan nominal, boundary, degraded, denial, deception, failover, holdover and recovery evaluation scenarios.</p></li>\n<li><p>Define measurable observables including error, uncertainty, availability, integrity state, detection time, failover time and recovery time.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why static and dynamic evaluation provide complementary evidence for resilient PNT.</p></li>\n<li><p>Use the PNT dependency chain to define verification scope and identify where evidence is required for external services, interfaces and shared dependencies.</p></li>\n<li><p>Assess coverage across threats, environments, configurations, sources, interfaces and operational modes.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise the importance of reproducibility, calibration, configuration control, logging and provenance.</p></li>\n<li><p>Build a simple PNT assurance argument linking claims to evidence, assumptions and residual risk.</p></li>\n<li><p>Challenge supplier test reports and resilience claims using evidence-led engineering questions.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m09-scope-and-safety\">Scope and safety</h2>\n<p>This module focuses on defensive verification and assurance. Testing of intentional RF interference or deceptive navigation signals must only be conducted using authorised facilities, lawful simulation, recorded data or suitably controlled test equipment. It does not provide instructions for transmitting interference or counterfeit navigation signals.</p>"}, {"id": "verification-validation-and-assurance-are-different", "title": "Verification, Validation and Assurance Are Different", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Concept</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Core question</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Typical evidence</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Verification</td>\n<td>Did we build the system or requirement correctly?</td>\n<td>Inspection, analysis, test results, interface checks, traceability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Validation</td>\n<td>Did we build the right capability for the intended mission and environment?</td>\n<td>Operational scenarios, representative trials, user evaluation, end-to-end evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assurance</td>\n<td>Is there a justified body of evidence supporting confidence in the critical claims?</td>\n<td>Structured argument combining requirements, risk analysis, V&amp;V results, limitations and residual risk.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineer insight</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>A system can be verified against a poor requirement and still be unsuitable for the mission. Validation checks the intended use. Assurance then asks whether the combined evidence is sufficient to justify trust in the claim being made.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "the-requirements-to-evidence-chain", "title": "The Requirements-to-Evidence Chain", "html": "<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The Requirements-to-Evidence Chain\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m09-image1.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<p>Every important PNT claim should be traceable backwards to a need and forwards to evidence. If a supplier claims “resilient positioning”, the engineer should be able to identify the relevant use case, threat model, performance requirement, verification method, test conditions, results and limitations.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-example-trace\">3.1 Example trace</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Element</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Worked example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Mission need</td>\n<td>Surface platform must retain safe navigation through a temporary GNSS outage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Requirement</td>\n<td>Following loss of GNSS, horizontal position error shall remain ≤ 20 m for at least 120 s while operating within the defined dynamics envelope.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Threat / condition</td>\n<td>Complete loss of usable GNSS measurements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Verification</td>\n<td>Controlled dynamic test using recorded/simulated GNSS loss plus independent reference truth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Observables</td>\n<td>Horizontal error, uncertainty estimate, source state, operating mode, alert time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Acceptance</td>\n<td>Error remains ≤ 20 m for 120 s; GNSS loss detected within defined time; no invalid “trusted” state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Time-aligned logs, reference truth, configuration baseline, plots, pass/fail record and anomalies.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assurance claim</td>\n<td>The tested configuration demonstrated the specified 120 s GNSS-outage bridging requirement under the stated conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "iadt-four-verification-methods", "title": "IADT: Four Verification Methods", "html": "<p>The supplied RethinkPNT material describes four common methods of verification: Inspection, Analysis, Demonstration and Testing. A mature strategy uses the method, or combination of methods, that provides the strongest evidence for the requirement being assessed.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Method</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What it does</strong></th>\n<th><strong>PNT example</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limitation if used alone</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Inspection</td>\n<td>Examines implementation and engineering artefacts.</td>\n<td>Review architecture to confirm diverse sources and no undocumented shared antenna path.</td>\n<td>Shows design/implementation evidence, but not necessarily runtime behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Analysis</td>\n<td>Uses calculations, models, data or structured reasoning.</td>\n<td>Predict INS drift or clock holdover against outage duration.</td>\n<td>Confidence depends on assumptions, model validity and input data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Demonstration</td>\n<td>Shows implemented capability operating successfully.</td>\n<td>Demonstrate controlled transition from GNSS to alternate navigation.</td>\n<td>Can favour success paths and may not expose boundaries or failure cases.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Executes known inputs and preconditions against expected measurable behaviour.</td>\n<td>Remove a PNT source and measure detection, failover, error growth and recovery.</td>\n<td>Coverage is limited to conditions actually exercised unless combined with other evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why combine methods</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>DHS resilience guidance similarly distinguishes static evaluation, such as architecture or code review, from dynamic evaluation using test vectors. Some resilience behaviours are best observed dynamically, while architectural properties such as genuine source diversity may need inspection and analysis as well.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "start-with-testable-requirements", "title": "Start with Testable Requirements", "html": "<p>Verification quality is constrained by requirement quality. “The system shall provide resilient PNT” is not directly testable. A useful requirement defines the capability, performance threshold, condition, duration and expected behaviour.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-a-practical-requirement-pattern\">5.1 A practical requirement pattern</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Requirement pattern</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>WHEN [defined PNT condition or threat occurs], THE SYSTEM SHALL [behaviour] WITHIN [response time], WHILE MAINTAINING [performance / integrity threshold] FOR [duration], AND SHALL [recovery behaviour] WHEN [recovery criteria are met].</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m09-weak-versus-verifiable\">5.2 Weak versus verifiable</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Weak statement</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Improved engineering statement</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>System shall tolerate GNSS loss.</td>\n<td>Following complete GNSS loss, the navigation solution shall remain within the mission-defined horizontal error limit for at least the specified bridging interval.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>System shall detect spoofing.</td>\n<td>When GNSS becomes inconsistent with the defined independent references beyond the specified threshold, the system shall declare GNSS suspect within the required detection time and prevent it from being presented as trusted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Timing shall be resilient.</td>\n<td>Following loss of the primary external time reference, time error shall remain within the defined tolerance for the specified holdover duration and shall be reported with an uncertainty/state indicator.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>System shall recover automatically.</td>\n<td>A returning source shall not be reinstated as trusted until defined revalidation criteria have been continuously satisfied for the specified interval.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "define-reference-truth-before-measuring-error", "title": "Define Reference Truth Before Measuring Error", "html": "<p>Accuracy cannot be demonstrated without a reference that is sufficiently better understood than the system under test. The reference does not have to be perfect, but its uncertainty must be known and appropriate for the measurement being claimed.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Quantity under test</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Possible reference truth</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Engineering considerations</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Position / trajectory</td>\n<td>Surveyed control point, high-grade reference GNSS/INS, optical or other independent trajectory reference.</td>\n<td>Reference accuracy, datum/frame, lever arms, antenna locations, synchronisation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Velocity / dynamics</td>\n<td>High-grade INS, calibrated motion platform, independent velocity reference.</td>\n<td>Time alignment, sampling rate, coordinate axes, dynamic range.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Time / frequency</td>\n<td>Traceable reference clock, calibrated time-transfer system, laboratory standard.</td>\n<td>Time scale, traceability, cable delays, oscillator uncertainty, measurement bandwidth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Integrity / source state</td>\n<td>Injected known condition plus independent observation of when it begins/ends.</td>\n<td>Exact event timing, expected alarm state, false alarm and missed detection criteria.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Measurement uncertainty matters</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>If the claimed performance is close to the uncertainty of the reference equipment or test setup, the evidence may not support the conclusion. Record calibration state, reference uncertainty and any corrections applied.</p></div></aside>\n<p><strong>Time and frequency measurement</strong></p>\n<p>Time and frequency measurements can be particularly sensitive to the measurement chain. The uncertainty associated with the reference clock, time-transfer mechanism, cabling, connectors, instrumentation, timestamping and measurement interval can all contribute to the observed result.</p>\n<p>The measurement system should therefore be sufficiently capable to distinguish the performance being claimed from the uncertainty introduced by the test setup itself.</p>\n<p>For timing assurance, ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>What time scale or frequency reference is being used?</p></li>\n<li><p>Is the reference traceable and appropriately calibrated?</p></li>\n<li><p>What uncertainty does the reference contribute?</p></li>\n<li><p>Have cable, interface and transfer delays been characterised?</p></li>\n<li><p>Over what measurement or averaging interval was performance assessed?</p></li>\n<li><p>Are environmental conditions relevant to the result?</p></li>\n<li><p>Is the measurement uncertainty sufficiently small relative to the acceptance threshold?</p></li>\n<li></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineering Principle</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>A precise-looking measurement is not automatically strong evidence. The engineer must understand the uncertainty of the measurement chain before deciding whether the result supports the assurance claim.</p></div></aside>\n<p>This is especially important when verifying clock stability or holdover. If a system is required to remain within a specified timing error for a defined outage duration, the test setup must be capable of demonstrating that performance with sufficient confidence throughout the relevant interval.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Define Reference Truth Before Measuring Error\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m09-image2.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "build-a-pnt-verification-matrix", "title": "Build a PNT Verification Matrix", "html": "<p>A verification matrix prevents assurance from becoming a collection of disconnected test reports. Each requirement should identify how it will be verified, under what conditions, using which observables and what evidence will be retained.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Req ID</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Requirement / claim</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Method</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Key observables</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Acceptance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-R-01</td>\n<td>Nominal horizontal accuracy</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Representative open-sky route</td>\n<td>Horizontal error, uncertainty</td>\n<td>≤ mission threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-R-07</td>\n<td>GNSS outage bridging</td>\n<td>Test + analysis</td>\n<td>GNSS unavailable for 120 s</td>\n<td>Error growth, mode, uncertainty</td>\n<td>Within threshold for duration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-R-11</td>\n<td>Independent source diversity</td>\n<td>Inspection + analysis</td>\n<td>Architecture baseline</td>\n<td>Source/dependency paths</td>\n<td>No prohibited common dependency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>PNT-R-14</td>\n<td>Misleading-source detection</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>GNSS inconsistent with independent truth</td>\n<td>Detection time, source state, output error</td>\n<td>Alert/isolate within threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PNT-R-18</td>\n<td>Controlled recovery</td>\n<td>Test + demonstration</td>\n<td>Primary source returns</td>\n<td>Revalidation state/time, output continuity</td>\n<td>No premature trust; recovery within limit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "test-environment-and-representativeness", "title": "Test Environment and Representativeness", "html": "<p>PNT performance is strongly dependent on environment. A result from one benign laboratory or open-sky condition should not be presented as universal evidence. The test environment must represent the conditions that matter to the use case or explicitly state what it does not cover.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-environment-dimensions\">8.1 Environment dimensions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>RF and satellite visibility: open sky, obstruction, multipath, indoor/covered areas and representative interference conditions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Platform dynamics: stationary, low/high speed, acceleration, turns, vibration and attitude changes.</p></li>\n<li><p>Atmosphere and geography: ionospheric/tropospheric effects where relevant, terrain, urban canyon, maritime or airborne environment.</p></li>\n<li><p>Communications: latency, packet loss, correction-service interruption, network path changes and bandwidth constraints.</p></li>\n<li><p>Power and hardware state: restart, brownout, antenna/cable faults, sensor disconnection and degraded components.</p></li>\n<li><p>Software/configuration: production firmware, configuration baseline, reference frames, time scales and interface versions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Operational duration: enough time to expose drift, holdover, convergence, thermal effects and recovery behaviour.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m09-simulation-laboratory-and-field-evidence\">8.2 Simulation, laboratory and field evidence</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Environment</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Strength</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Limitation</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Good use</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Simulation / recorded data</td>\n<td>Repeatable and safe; precise event control.</td>\n<td>Model/data realism may be limited.</td>\n<td>Regression, boundary conditions, source-loss/deception scenarios.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Laboratory / hardware-in-loop</td>\n<td>Controlled, instrumented and reproducible.</td>\n<td>May not reproduce full operational environment.</td>\n<td>Receiver/system behaviour, interfaces, timing, fault injection.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Field trial</td>\n<td>High operational realism.</td>\n<td>Harder to repeat/control; truth may be difficult.</td>\n<td>Validation of end-to-end mission behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Operational exercise</td>\n<td>Real users, procedures and system interactions.</td>\n<td>Expensive; limited threat control; evidence collection challenging.</td>\n<td>Final validation, human/system response and procedures.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "nominal-performance-is-only-the-baseline", "title": "Nominal Performance Is Only the Baseline", "html": "<p>Start by establishing normal behaviour and measurement variability. Without a baseline, it is difficult to distinguish a resilience failure from ordinary system performance.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Performance area</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example observables</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Accuracy</td>\n<td>Horizontal/vertical position error, velocity error, timing offset.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Precision / repeatability</td>\n<td>Scatter, variance, repeat-run distribution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Availability</td>\n<td>Percentage of required operation in which a usable solution is supplied.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Continuity</td>\n<td>Unexpected interruptions during the required operational interval.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Integrity</td>\n<td>Alert state, protection/uncertainty information, hazardous misleading information, time to alert.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Latency / freshness</td>\n<td>Age of measurement, processing delay, correction age, end-to-end delivery delay.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Convergence / start-up</td>\n<td>Time to first usable solution, RTK fix/convergence, post-recovery convergence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Resource / system behaviour</td>\n<td>CPU/load where relevant, logging, communications, power and thermal effects.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "resilience-testing-exercise-the-failure-behaviour", "title": "Resilience Testing: Exercise the Failure Behaviour", "html": "<p>Resilience testing should deliberately exercise the conditions the architecture was designed to tolerate. The objective is not merely to prove that an alternate source exists, but to observe resistance, detection, isolation, transition, degraded performance, user notification and recovery.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Resilience Testing: Exercise the Failure Behaviour\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m09-image3.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m09-section-1\"></h2>\n<h2 id=\"m09-core-scenario-families\">10.1 Core scenario families</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Scenario family</strong></th>\n<th><strong>What to evaluate</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Typical observables</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Source denial / loss</td>\n<td>Behaviour when GNSS, correction, network time or another source disappears.</td>\n<td>Detection time, availability, failover time, error growth, mode.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Degradation</td>\n<td>Behaviour as source quality worsens before total loss.</td>\n<td>Uncertainty, thresholds, alerts, performance trend.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Misleading source</td>\n<td>Whether plausible but inconsistent data is detected and prevented from contaminating trusted output.</td>\n<td>Residuals, cross-source disagreement, time to alarm, isolation state, output error.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Common-mode failure</td>\n<td>Whether nominally redundant paths fail together.</td>\n<td>Shared dependency behaviour, remaining independent capability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Holdover / bridging</td>\n<td>Performance while external reference is absent.</td>\n<td>Error versus time, uncertainty growth, duration within tolerance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Failover</td>\n<td>Controlled transition to alternate source/mode.</td>\n<td>Transition time, transient error, continuity, downstream status.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Revalidation and reintegration after disturbance ends.</td>\n<td>Recovery time, revalidation evidence, state reset, transient behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interface / data failure</td>\n<td>Malformed, stale, delayed, inconsistent or missing PNT data.</td>\n<td>Validation, rejection, alerting, safe behaviour, logging.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "detection-performance-needs-its-own-metrics", "title": "Detection Performance Needs Its Own Metrics", "html": "<p>A system that eventually notices a bad source may still fail the mission. Detection must therefore be measured, not simply observed.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Metric</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Question</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Time to detect / time to alert</td>\n<td>How long after the adverse condition begins does the system declare a problem?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Missed detection</td>\n<td>How often does a defined harmful condition occur without the required alert?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>False alarm</td>\n<td>How often is a healthy source incorrectly declared suspect?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Isolation correctness</td>\n<td>Does the system identify the affected source or merely know that sources disagree?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Output exposure</td>\n<td>How much erroneous PNT reaches the application before isolation?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Alert propagation</td>\n<td>Does the consuming application/operator receive the correct integrity and mode state in time?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Important trade-off</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>Thresholds that are too sensitive can create nuisance alarms and unnecessary failovers. Thresholds that are too permissive can allow hazardous misleading information to persist. The correct balance is mission and risk dependent.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "holdover-and-bridging-tests", "title": "Holdover and Bridging Tests", "html": "<p>Holdover is inherently time-dependent. The test should therefore measure error and uncertainty as a function of outage duration, environmental conditions and platform dynamics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-timing-example\">12.1 Timing example</h2>\n<p>Requirement: following loss of the primary time reference, the system shall maintain UTC-relative time error within ±1 μs for 24 hours under the specified temperature range.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Test element</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Precondition</td>\n<td>Clock disciplined and stable; configuration and calibration recorded.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Event</td>\n<td>Remove primary external reference at known time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Truth</td>\n<td>Independent traceable time reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Observe</td>\n<td>Time offset, frequency offset, uncertainty estimate, holdover state and alarms.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Duration</td>\n<td>At least 24 hours plus margin sufficient to observe boundary behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Pass/fail</td>\n<td>Absolute time error remains within 1 μs for required duration and status correctly reports holdover.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>Restore reference; measure reacquisition, revalidation and any transient/time step.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"m09-navigation-bridging-example\">12.2 Navigation bridging example</h2>\n<p>For an INS-supported platform, repeat GNSS-outage tests across representative dynamics. A stationary result does not establish performance during manoeuvre. Plot position error and reported uncertainty against time since GNSS loss, then compare both against mission thresholds.</p>"}, {"id": "recovery-testing-is-more-than-restoring-the-source", "title": "Recovery Testing Is More Than Restoring the Source", "html": "<p>Module 7 established that a returning source should not automatically become trusted. Recovery tests should verify that contaminated state is cleared or bounded, the source is revalidated, the system transitions in a controlled manner and downstream users receive the correct status.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Recovery question</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Evidence to seek</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>How is return detected?</td>\n<td>Source-state logs and event timing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>What proves the source is trustworthy again?</td>\n<td>Consistency checks, authentication/integrity indicators where applicable, independent cross-checks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>What internal state persists?</td>\n<td>Architecture/code inspection plus state/log evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>How long until normal performance returns?</td>\n<td>Recovery time and convergence measurements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Can recovery create a transient hazard?</td>\n<td>Output plots across reintegration, discontinuity checks and application behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Does the system learn or change configuration?</td>\n<td>Configuration/change records, controlled baseline and regression evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "negative-boundary-and-fault-injection-testing", "title": "Negative, Boundary and Fault-Injection Testing", "html": "<p>Positive testing shows that expected inputs produce expected outputs. Resilience assurance also needs deliberate examination of invalid, missing, stale, contradictory or out-of-range inputs and failures at interfaces.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Remove one source, then combinations of sources justified by the threat model.</p></li>\n<li><p>Interrupt correction or network services and vary latency/age beyond nominal limits.</p></li>\n<li><p>Provide stale, malformed or inconsistent data at software interfaces in a controlled test environment.</p></li>\n<li><p>Exercise start-up with missing sources and recovery after power interruption.</p></li>\n<li><p>Cross thresholds slowly to observe hysteresis and mode-chatter behaviour.</p></li>\n<li><p>Force disagreement between independent sources using authorised simulation or recorded data.</p></li>\n<li><p>Test invalid configuration, reference-frame mismatch and time-scale mismatch where credible.</p></li>\n<li><p>Repeat after firmware/software changes to demonstrate regression control.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Safety boundary</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>For RF denial/deception, prefer lawful simulators, screened facilities, recorded signal/data replay or approved ranges. Never radiate intentional interference into uncontrolled environments.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "static-evaluation-inspect-what-dynamic-tests-cannot-prove", "title": "Static Evaluation: Inspect What Dynamic Tests Cannot Prove", "html": "<p>Some claims concern architecture rather than a single runtime response. A short dynamic test cannot prove that two sources are genuinely independent across all relevant failures. Static evidence can expose shared power, antenna, software, network, cloud, timing or supplier dependencies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-useful-static-evidence\">15.1 Useful static evidence</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>System and PNT architecture diagrams, including physical and logical interfaces.</p></li>\n<li><p>Dependency trees and data-flow diagrams.</p></li>\n<li><p>FMEA/FMECA, fault-tree or hazard analysis where appropriate.</p></li>\n<li><p>Threat model and misuse/abuse cases.</p></li>\n<li><p>Source provenance and upstream timing/reference dependencies.</p></li>\n<li><p>Interface control documents and data contracts.</p></li>\n<li><p>Configuration and software/firmware baselines.</p></li>\n<li><p>Supplier evidence, known limitations and change history.</p></li>\n<li><p>Calibration records and uncertainty budgets.</p></li>\n<li><p>Requirements traceability and verification matrix.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m09-verify-the-dependency-chain-not-only-the-receiver\">15.2 Verify the dependency chain, not only the receiver</h2>\n<p>Module 8 introduced the PNT dependency chain as a way of tracing the sources, infrastructure, interfaces and services on which a delivered PNT capability depends. Verification scope should follow that chain. Testing only the receiver can leave important system-level claims unsupported if the operational capability also depends on antennas, correction services, communications, local clocks, software, maps, reference data or downstream application behaviour.</p>\n<p>For each mission-critical PNT requirement, identify which parts of the dependency chain can cause the requirement to fail or become misleading, then decide what evidence is needed at each relevant point.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Upstream evidence: source provenance, reference and timing dependencies, correction services, antenna/RF paths, networks, infrastructure and third-party services.</p></li>\n<li><p>Internal evidence: receiver behaviour, monitoring, FDI, fusion, holdover, uncertainty estimation, mode management and recovery logic.</p></li>\n<li><p>Downstream evidence: whether integrity, uncertainty, alerts and degraded modes propagate correctly to the consuming application and are acted upon as intended.</p></li>\n<li><p>Common-mode evidence: inspection or analysis showing whether nominally diverse sources share power, timing, software, communications, data, supplier or infrastructure dependencies.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<p>This does not mean every external service must be owned or tested directly by the PNT team. It means the assurance argument must make the dependency visible and provide proportionate evidence, constraints, supplier assurance or explicit residual risk for the claim being made.</p>"}, {"id": "coverage-how-much-evidence-is-enough", "title": "Coverage: How Much Evidence Is Enough?", "html": "<p>No finite test programme exercises every combination. Coverage should therefore be risk-based and explicit. The engineer should be able to explain what was tested, what was analysed, what was inspected, what was demonstrated, and what remains an assumption or residual risk.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Coverage dimension</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Questions</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Requirements</td>\n<td>Has every critical PNT/resilience requirement got an agreed verification method and acceptance criterion?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Threats / failures</td>\n<td>Are credible high-consequence denial, degradation, deception and technical failures covered?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sources / dependencies</td>\n<td>Have primary, alternate and shared dependencies been exercised or analysed?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Operating modes</td>\n<td>Normal, degraded, restricted, holdover, failover, safe and recovery states?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Environment</td>\n<td>Representative RF, dynamics, communications, temperature, geography and duration?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Interfaces</td>\n<td>Correct, missing, stale, delayed, malformed and inconsistent data?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Configuration</td>\n<td>Production-representative hardware, firmware, software and settings?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Change / regression</td>\n<td>Are critical tests repeated after relevant changes?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>RIN has highlighted that there is no single universal set of resilience tests suitable for all UK PNT applications. Coverage must therefore be justified against the specific use case, requirements and risks rather than by claiming a generic resilience score.</p>"}, {"id": "reproducibility-configuration-and-evidence-quality", "title": "Reproducibility, Configuration and Evidence Quality", "html": "<p>A test that cannot be reconstructed is weak assurance evidence. Record enough context for another competent team to understand what was tested and, where practical, reproduce it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-minimum-evidence-package\">17.1 Minimum evidence package</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Requirement and test objective.</p></li>\n<li><p>Test case/scenario identifier and version.</p></li>\n<li><p>System-under-test hardware, software, firmware and configuration baseline.</p></li>\n<li><p>Test environment, equipment and calibration status.</p></li>\n<li><p>Reference truth and its uncertainty.</p></li>\n<li><p>Inputs, preconditions, event timing and threat/failure condition.</p></li>\n<li><p>Raw and processed observables with common time base.</p></li>\n<li><p>Expected results and pass/fail criteria defined before execution.</p></li>\n<li><p>Actual results, anomalies, deviations and retests.</p></li>\n<li><p>Operator actions and manual interventions.</p></li>\n<li><p>Limitations, assumptions and environmental differences from intended operation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Approvals/review and links back to requirements and risk items.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Evidence principle</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>Do not discard inconvenient anomalies simply because the final test passed. Unexpected behaviour can reveal a missing requirement, weak monitor, unmodelled dependency or incorrect assumption.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "worked-example-assuring-the-module-8-surface-platform", "title": "Worked Example: Assuring the Module 8 Surface Platform", "html": "<p>Recall the autonomous surface platform from Module 8. It uses GNSS, INS, speed/heading aids and integrity monitoring. The mission requires continued safe navigation through temporary GNSS loss and safe handling of misleading GNSS.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-verification-strategy\">18.1 Verification strategy</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Claim</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Method</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Scenario</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Evidence</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Nominal navigation meets accuracy requirement.</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Representative routes and dynamics with GNSS available.</td>\n<td>Truth-referenced error distributions and availability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>INS can bridge GNSS outage.</td>\n<td>Test + analysis</td>\n<td>GNSS removed for multiple durations/dynamics.</td>\n<td>Error growth vs time, uncertainty, model comparison.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>GNSS deception is detected.</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Authorised simulated/recorded GNSS becomes inconsistent with independent truth.</td>\n<td>Detection time, residuals, source state, output exposure.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Alternate sources are independent enough for target failures.</td>\n<td>Inspection + analysis</td>\n<td>Architecture/dependency review.</td>\n<td>Dependency map, common-mode analysis, supplier evidence.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Application degrades safely.</td>\n<td>Demonstration + test</td>\n<td>Uncertainty crosses normal/restricted/safe thresholds.</td>\n<td>Mode transitions, operator alerts, autonomy restrictions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Recovered GNSS is revalidated.</td>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>Primary source restored after suspect state.</td>\n<td>Revalidation state, recovery time, transient error, logs.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"m09-example-results-interpretation\">18.2 Example results interpretation</h2>\n<p>Suppose the platform stays within its 20 m requirement for 120 s of GNSS loss in straight-line motion but exceeds 20 m after 75 s during aggressive manoeuvres. The correct conclusion is not “GNSS outage resilience passed”. The evidence supports a narrower claim: the architecture meets the requirement only for the tested dynamics envelope, or the requirement/architecture needs refinement for manoeuvring conditions.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Assurance language</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>State what the evidence supports, not what you hoped to demonstrate. Bound every resilience claim by configuration, environment, threat condition, duration and performance.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "worked-example-misleading-gnss-and-failover", "title": "Worked Example: Misleading GNSS and Failover", "html": "<p>Scenario: GNSS remains available and reports plausible movement, but begins diverging from independent reference information. The system is expected to detect the inconsistency, isolate GNSS, continue in a degraded mode and later revalidate GNSS before reintegration.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Phase</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Expected behaviour</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Measure</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Baseline</td>\n<td>All sources consistent; GNSS trusted.</td>\n<td>Nominal residuals, source states, error.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Divergence begins</td>\n<td>Monitor detects growing inconsistency.</td>\n<td>Event time, residual trend.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Detection</td>\n<td>GNSS declared suspect within requirement.</td>\n<td>Time to detect/alert; false state transitions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Isolation</td>\n<td>Suspect GNSS excluded or down-weighted as designed.</td>\n<td>Output exposure, source weighting/state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Degraded operation</td>\n<td>Alternate sources maintain bounded capability.</td>\n<td>Error, uncertainty, availability, mode.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Threat removed</td>\n<td>GNSS returns to consistent measurements but remains under revalidation.</td>\n<td>Revalidation timer/criteria.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Recovery</td>\n<td>GNSS reintegrated without hazardous discontinuity.</td>\n<td>Recovery time, transient error, state/logs.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "building-a-pnt-assurance-case", "title": "Building a PNT Assurance Case", "html": "<p>The supplied RethinkPNT material distinguishes resilience from assurance. Assurance requires a documented body of evidence and a convincing argument that the system has the required critical properties for the intended application and environment. The assurance case is therefore the structure that explains why the evidence is sufficient.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m09-section-2\"><figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Building a PNT Assurance Case\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m09-image4.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure></h2>\n<h2 id=\"m09-example-assurance-argument\">20.1 Example assurance argument</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Element</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Example</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Top claim</td>\n<td>The platform provides sufficiently trustworthy navigation for the defined mission profile under the specified PNT threat conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Sub-claim 1</td>\n<td>Nominal PNT performance meets accuracy, availability and integrity requirements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sub-claim 2</td>\n<td>Defined source failures and GNSS loss are detected and managed without violating mission thresholds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Sub-claim 3</td>\n<td>Misleading GNSS is detected and isolated within the required exposure time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Sub-claim 4</td>\n<td>Degraded navigation and recovery behaviour meet defined requirements.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Evidence</td>\n<td>Verification matrix, test reports, truth data, architecture review, FMEA, calibration, field validation and regression results.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assumptions / limits</td>\n<td>Specified dynamics, environmental envelope, configuration, threat model, source availability and operator procedures.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Residual risk</td>\n<td>Known conditions not fully mitigated or tested, with ownership and operational treatment.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>An assurance case does not turn incomplete evidence into certainty. Its value is that claims, evidence, assumptions, limitations and residual risks are visible and reviewable.</p>"}, {"id": "challenging-supplier-evidence", "title": "Challenging Supplier Evidence", "html": "<p>A polished demonstration or headline accuracy figure is not the same as assurance. When reviewing supplier evidence, ask questions that reveal the boundaries of the claim.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Which exact application requirement does this result support?</p></li>\n<li><p>What system configuration, firmware and settings were tested?</p></li>\n<li><p>What was used as reference truth, and what is its uncertainty?</p></li>\n<li><p>Which threat/failure conditions, magnitudes and durations were exercised?</p></li>\n<li><p>What were the pre-defined pass/fail criteria?</p></li>\n<li><p>Were detection time, false alarms and missed detections measured?</p></li>\n<li><p>What happens to downstream applications while the source is suspect?</p></li>\n<li><p>Were failover, degraded operation and recovery tested end to end?</p></li>\n<li><p>How representative were platform dynamics and the RF/physical environment?</p></li>\n<li><p>What evidence supports source independence and common-mode claims?</p></li>\n<li><p>What anomalies, failures or exclusions occurred during testing?</p></li>\n<li><p>Can the results be reproduced, and what evidence is available for independent review?</p></li>\n<li><p>What has changed since the evidence was generated, and what regression evidence exists?</p></li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Useful wording</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>“Tested against spoofing” is incomplete. A defensible statement identifies the scenario, test method, threat assumptions, detection criteria, performance during the event, recovery behaviour and limitations.</p></div></aside>"}, {"id": "common-assurance-anti-patterns", "title": "Common Assurance Anti-Patterns", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Anti-pattern</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why it is weak</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Only nominal accuracy is tested.</td>\n<td>Does not demonstrate behaviour when PNT is unavailable, degraded or misleading.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Pass/fail criteria are chosen after seeing results.</td>\n<td>Creates confirmation bias and weakens objectivity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>One successful demonstration is treated as validation.</td>\n<td>Does not establish repeatability, boundaries or failure behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>No independent truth source.</td>\n<td>Accuracy/error claims cannot be objectively substantiated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Receiver is tested but consuming application is ignored.</td>\n<td>Integrity/mode information may not propagate or be acted upon correctly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Failover is tested, recovery is not.</td>\n<td>Returning sources may be trusted prematurely or create hazardous transients.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Redundant sources are assumed independent.</td>\n<td>Common dependencies can invalidate the resilience claim.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Simulation result is presented as operational proof.</td>\n<td>Simulation is valuable evidence but its representativeness must be justified.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Evidence has no configuration baseline.</td>\n<td>Results may not apply to the deployed system.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>“Certified” is treated as proof for every use case.</td>\n<td>Conformance to a standard or framework must still be mapped to the application requirement and operating context.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>Verification asks whether requirements have been implemented correctly; validation asks whether the resulting capability is suitable for its intended use; assurance justifies confidence using a body of evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p>IADT provides four complementary verification methods: Inspection, Analysis, Demonstration and Test.</p></li>\n<li><p>Every critical PNT claim should trace from mission need and requirement through verification method, acceptance criteria and retained evidence.</p></li>\n<li><p>PNT error and integrity claims require appropriate reference truth, time alignment and understood measurement uncertainty.</p></li>\n<li><p>Resilience evaluation must exercise denial, degradation, misleading information, failover, holdover, degraded modes and controlled recovery where relevant to the threat model.</p></li>\n<li><p>Detection requires measurable criteria such as time to alert, false alarms, missed detections and erroneous-output exposure.</p></li>\n<li><p>Static architecture/dependency evidence complements dynamic testing and is essential for many common-mode and independence claims.</p></li>\n<li><p>Verification scope should follow the end-to-end PNT dependency chain so that upstream services, internal processing and downstream application behaviour are covered where they can affect the mission claim.</p></li>\n<li><p>Test coverage should be risk-based and explicit across requirements, threats, environments, modes, interfaces, configurations and changes.</p></li>\n<li><p>Reproducibility depends on controlled configuration, calibrated equipment, raw evidence, defined criteria and documented limitations.</p></li>\n<li><p>A PNT assurance case connects claims to evidence, assumptions and residual risk rather than claiming absolute resilience.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Verification</td>\n<td>Objective confirmation that specified requirements have been fulfilled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Validation</td>\n<td>Confirmation that the system is suitable for its intended use and operational need.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Assurance</td>\n<td>Justified confidence, supported by a documented body of evidence and argument, that critical properties are satisfied.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>IADT</td>\n<td>Inspection, Analysis, Demonstration and Test, four common verification methods.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reference truth</td>\n<td>Independent reference used to quantify the error/performance of the system under evaluation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Observable</td>\n<td>A measurable state, signal, output or event used to evaluate behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Acceptance criterion</td>\n<td>Predefined condition used to determine whether a requirement/test passes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Test vector</td>\n<td>Defined input/data sequence used to exercise and evaluate system behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Time to alert</td>\n<td>Elapsed time from the onset/detection condition to the required integrity alert.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Holdover</td>\n<td>Continued timing operation after loss of an external reference, with uncertainty generally increasing over time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Coverage</td>\n<td>Extent to which requirements, conditions, risks, configurations and behaviours have been evaluated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Assurance case</td>\n<td>Structured argument connecting claims to evidence, assumptions, context and residual risk.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Residual risk</td>\n<td>Risk remaining after controls, verification and mitigations have been considered.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Regression testing</td>\n<td>Re-execution of relevant tests after change to confirm previously demonstrated behaviour remains valid.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Verification scope</td>\n<td>The system elements, dependencies, interfaces, conditions and behaviours for which evidence is required to support a stated claim.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Measurement uncertainty</td>\n<td>Quantified doubt associated with a measurement result, including relevant contributions from the reference, instrumentation, transfer path, method and test conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p><strong>DHS / CISA: Resilient PNT Conformance Framework v2.0</strong></p>\n<p>Outcome-based resilience framework with evaluation coverage, static/dynamic analysis and example test-plan elements. DHS describes the framework as source-agnostic and outcome-based, with four resilience levels.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2022-05/22_0531_st_resilient_pnt_conformance_framework_v2.0.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>DHS S&amp;T: Best Practices for Resilient PNT Supporting Critical Infrastructure</strong></p>\n<p>Current best-practice guidance including qualification, testing, reproducible test methods and evaluation of foreseeable adverse PNT events. The guidance specifically includes creating test plans for foreseeable adverse PNT events and defining reproducible PNT test suites.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2025-02/25_0220_st_pnt_best_practices_ci.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dhs.gov/sites/default/files/2025-02/25_0220_st_pnt_best_practices_ci.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>DHS S&amp;T: GNSS Test Vector Suite and Distribution Methodology</strong></p>\n<p>Resource supporting repeatable GNSS scenarios and standards conformity assessment. DHS launched the suite as a way for infrastructure operators to independently define test scenarios and evaluate GNSS/PNT equipment using standardised simulated test data.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.dhs.gov/science-and-technology/news/2025/05/19/st-releases-new-tool-strengthen-global-navigation-satellite-systems\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.dhs.gov/science-and-technology/news/2025/05/19/st-releases-new-tool-strengthen-global-navigation-satellite-systems</a></p>\n<p><strong>NIST IR 8323 Revision 2, Foundational PNT Profile (2026 draft)</strong></p>\n<p>Risk-management guidance covering PNT dependencies, protection, anomaly detection, response, recovery, testing and third-party risks. This is the <strong>May 2026 Initial Public Draft</strong>, aligned to NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0, rather than a final publication.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/ir/8323/r2/ipd</a></p>\n<p><strong>RethinkPNT: A Structured Approach to Achieving System Resilience for PNT Systems</strong><br/>\nCourse source covering the structured PNT resilience approach, including systems engineering, verification and assurance concepts used within the Daintta programme.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rethinkpnt.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/Website_copy_PNT-System-Resilience.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: Resilient PNT Resources Portal</strong></p>\n<p>UK resource hub containing resilient PNT best practices, the PNT Resilience Checklist, practical guidance, training resources and case studies.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/mpage/Resilient_PNT</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: PNT Test and Evaluation training</strong></p>\n<p>The RIN SALHUB PNT programme includes the dedicated PNT-010A Test and Evaluation of PNT Systems module, alongside related systems engineering, sensor integration and assured traceable timing modules.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/event/SALHUBOnlinePNT\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/event/SALHUBOnlinePNT</a></p>\n<p><strong>National Physical Laboratory (NPL): Introduction to Clock Performance</strong></p>\n<p>Additional training covering clock comparison, measurement techniques, stability measures, uncertainty budgets and interpretation of clock-performance evidence. Particularly relevant when verifying timing and holdover requirements.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://training.npl.co.uk/product/introduction-to-clock-performance/\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://training.npl.co.uk/product/introduction-to-clock-performance/</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 10 — APPLIED PNT ENGINEERING</p><div class=\"callout-body\"><p></p>\n<p>The final module brings the programme together through realistic scenarios. Learners will identify PNT dependencies, derive requirements, analyse threats, design a resilient architecture, define degraded/failover/recovery behaviour and specify the verification and assurance evidence needed to justify the solution.</p></div></aside>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "Which statement best distinguishes verification from validation?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Verification checks implementation against requirements; validation checks suitability for intended use."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Verification is only laboratory testing; validation is only field testing."}, {"id": "C", "text": "They are interchangeable terms."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Validation only applies to software."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Verification addresses conformance to requirements; validation addresses fitness for intended mission/use."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Which are recognised verification methods in this module?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Inspection"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Analysis"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Demonstration"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Test"}, {"id": "E", "text": "Marketing review"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "IADT provides complementary ways of generating verification evidence."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "Why is reference truth important?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "It guarantees the system will never fail."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It provides a basis for quantifying error and performance."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It removes the need for calibration."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It is only required for timing systems."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Error is meaningful only relative to a suitable reference whose uncertainty is understood."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "Which should normally be defined before a resilience test?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Requirement/claim"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Test condition and preconditions"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Observables"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Pass/fail criteria"}, {"id": "E", "text": "Configuration baseline"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Objective evaluation requires the claim, conditions, measurements, criteria and tested configuration to be controlled."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "A platform meets its outage requirement while stationary but fails during manoeuvres. What is the strongest conclusion?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The whole resilience claim passes."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The whole system is unusable."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Evidence supports only the conditions in which the requirement was met; the dynamics boundary needs addressing."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Ignore the manoeuvring test as an outlier."}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "The claim must be bounded by the evidence. Dynamics are part of the operating envelope."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "Which are useful deception-detection metrics?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Time to detect"}, {"id": "B", "text": "False alarm rate"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Missed detection behaviour"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Output exposure before isolation"}, {"id": "E", "text": "Alert propagation to the application"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Detection assurance includes speed, correctness and how much harmful data reaches the user."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "Why combine static and dynamic evaluation?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Static review can assess architectural properties that a finite dynamic test may not prove, while dynamic testing observes runtime behaviour."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Dynamic testing is never useful."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Static analysis always proves operational performance."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It is only needed when requirements are missing."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Architecture/dependency claims and runtime behaviour often require different evidence methods."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "What should recovery testing examine?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Revalidation before trust is restored"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Recovery time"}, {"id": "C", "text": "Transient behaviour during reintegration"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Correct downstream status"}, {"id": "E", "text": "Whether contaminated state persists"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Recovery is a controlled re-establishment of trusted operation, not simply source return."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "What is the purpose of a PNT assurance case?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "To replace testing with documentation."}, {"id": "B", "text": "To provide a structured argument linking critical claims to evidence, assumptions and residual risk."}, {"id": "C", "text": "To guarantee immunity to every threat."}, {"id": "D", "text": "To provide a marketing score."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Assurance makes the reasoning from evidence to claim explicit, including assumptions and residual risk."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "Which weaken supplier assurance evidence?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Unknown reference-truth uncertainty"}, {"id": "B", "text": "Undefined threat conditions"}, {"id": "C", "text": "No configuration baseline"}, {"id": "D", "text": "Pass/fail criteria defined after testing"}, {"id": "E", "text": "No evidence of degraded/recovery behaviour"}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "Each makes it harder to know what was demonstrated and whether it applies to the deployed use case."}]});
